{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795360078,"nonce":"vNui0PY1cnM2ZGeTDNGALiznTKM=","title":"Std.parseInt of the lowest int panics in a debug build of ash_std","message":"hlp_parse_int negated the parsed value with `h = -h`, in the decimal branch and in the hex branch. For \"-2147483648\" (and \"-0x80000000\") the accumulated value is already i32::MIN, so the negation overflows. A release build wraps and returns i32::MIN, which is what HashLink returns. A debug build, where overflow checks are on, panics with \"attempt to negate with overflow\", and because the primitive cannot unwind the process aborts with SIGABRT.\n\nSeen in the nested_strip integration tests, whose program parses \"-2147483648\". They only passed locally while the debug runtime embedded in ash was stale and built without overflow checks. CI does not run nested_strip, so nothing flagged it.\n\nFix: wrapping_neg in both branches, with a unit test that parses the lowest int in both forms.","files":null}]}